docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:57:## Negative Controls
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:59:- `evidence-candidate` artifacts can inform intake and planning only.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:60:- `local-diagnostic` artifacts can explain status but cannot set
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:61:  `productionDone`, even if they contain `accepted=true`.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:62:- `local-key-single-host` artifacts are local/offline evidence and cannot be
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:63:  promoted to Authority proof.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:64:- `local-gate-evidence` artifacts can satisfy their local lifecycle readiness
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:65:  claim but cannot satisfy `production-completion`.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:66:- `local-review-quorum` can satisfy local review escalation only.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:67:- `legacy-observed` artifacts remain audit-readable but are not strong proof.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:68:- `authority-receipt` must not be confused with `production-gate-decision`.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:69:- `production-gate-decision` is admissible only for a matching consumer and
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:70:  claim, with current schema, shape, digest, receipt/signature, subject, and
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:71:  temporal/material bindings.
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:75:`caller-writable` surfaces are never trusted for completion claims. `local-runtime-owned`
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:76:surfaces can be useful current-state or diagnostic evidence but are not
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:77:Authority-owned. `trusted-reviewer-owned` surfaces can satisfy local REVIEW
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:78:policies. `authority-owned` surfaces can become production eligible only after
docs/evidence/proof-surface-inventory.md:79:the matching gate decision/ref path has classified and consumed them.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:3:## Decision 1: flow functions must have @test
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:5:A function annotated with @flow is on a business-critical path and must have a @test comment pointing to a fixture.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:6:During `task_scan` validation, a flow function missing @test should report an error rather than being silently ignored.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:8:**Rationale**: A problem in a flow function affects the entire chain, so it ranks higher in priority than an ordinary function. Previously `gap_analyze` reported every `no_test` uniformly as medium, which drowned out the critical paths.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:10:## Decision 2: flow chains must have end-to-end verification
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:12:Every @flow chain needs an end-to-end test — run the full pipeline with temporary data and verify that the data is carried from start to finish.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:13:When `test_auto` modifies any function within a flow, it automatically triggers the end-to-end regression for that flow.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:15:**Rationale**: A passing single-function test does not mean the chain is sound. Data hand-off between functions and state dependencies can only be covered end-to-end.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:17:## Decision 3: task_scan should perform a full code health check
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:19:`task_scan` currently only validates comment formatting. It should additionally include:
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:20:- A @test missing check (for every comment that has a functionName)
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:21:- Reporting an error for a flow function that has no test (higher than for an ordinary function)
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:23:**Rationale**: While the AI is developing, it should be able to call `task_scan` at any time and learn "what is still missing at the code level," rather than discovering it only at `gap_analyze` time.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:25:## Decision 4: gap_analyze layered presentation
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:28:- A flow function missing a test → severity: high
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:29:- An ordinary function missing a test → severity: medium
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:31:**Rationale**: A flat list of 39 medium items is not actionable. Once layered, the AI knows which ones to fill in first.
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:33:## Policy: test-evidence.json is gate-consumed validation metadata
docs/requirements/decisions/testing-completeness.md:35:test-evidence.json is gate-consumed validation metadata, not material drift input. PASS/FAIL/coverage results still feed gates (TEST→DONE freshness check), and real source or evidence drift remains stale. Changes to test-evidence.json alone do not invalidate prior material-hash evidence.
docs/guide.md:1:# Project Documentation Navigation
docs/guide.md:7:This project uses software engineering to manage the automated generation, optimization, and maintenance of a complete programming project by AI. It builds large-model development orchestration around the Codex CLI, Claude Code CLI, Antigravity CLI (`agy`), and LLM APIs, with Gemini retained only as a deprecated legacy compatibility host, plugging the right large model into the right scenario and making the best decisions across four dimensions: development needs, cost, efficiency, and production quality. It forms a closed development loop from a stakeholder raising a requirement, to the requirement landing as documentation, to task breakdown, and finally to delivery. The end goal is a system that can be maintained over the long term and that self-tunes and self-upgrades automatically.
docs/guide.md:15:* Resource consumption must be measurable; dimensions can include CPU time and tokens (if those are unavailable, use the number of FunctionCall invocations, with a minimum of 1).
docs/guide.md:16:* It must produce and maintain data (including code, documentation, logs, and so on); without that, it cannot exist on its own.
docs/guide.md:17:* Total output measurement: how much documentation, how much code, how many bugs were reported. Actual output measurement: how many bugs the code produced, and how many of those bugs were genuinely valid.
docs/guide.md:18:* Bug distillation: every bug must be recorded in detail—which role reported it, the time, the related code, the Git revision, the cause, the handling process (the solution), the resolution outcome, and keywords (so it can be quickly located on reproduction).
docs/guide.md:19:* Sub Agent evaluation: it must record in detail how many resources each Agent consumed, how much content it produced, and how much of that content was valid.
docs/guide.md:20:* The entire development process and the Story lifecycle are managed by the program, with the underlying Agent or LLM reasoning making the decisions. Advancing the development process is like breathing—it cannot stop or err (and is relatively fixed), so it must not be left to the Agent to decide.
docs/guide.md:21:* Every Sub Agent must be tunable: its context, scope of responsibility, workload, and whether it participates must all be dynamically adjustable. Each Story's development cadence must be dynamically adjusted according to the system's runtime and monitoring state, never rigidly fixed.
